\begin{proposition}[Classical-MDS truncation] Assume that $\B$ has at least $p$ positive eigenvalues.  Then \begin{equation}  \min_{\X\in\R^{N\times p}}\|\B-\X\X^{\top}\|_{F}^{2}  =\sum_{a>p:\,\lambda_a>0}\lambda_a^2   +\sum_{a:\,\lambda_a<0}\lambda_a^2,  \label{eq:psdapprox} \end{equation} and a minimizer is \begin{equation}  \X_p=\begin{bmatrix}\bm v_1&\cdots&\bm v_p\end{bmatrix}        \diag(\sqrt{\lambda_1},\ldots,\sqrt{\lambda_p}).  \label{eq:cmds} \end{equation} If $\lambda_p>\max(\lambda_{p+1},0)$, the minimizing Gram matrix is unique; its coordinate factor is unique up to right multiplication by an element of $O(p)$.  A degeneracy at the retained/omitted boundary makes the retained subspace nonunique. \end{proposition}
\begin{proof} Every $\X\X^{\top}$ is positive semidefinite with rank at most $p$. Orthogonal invariance of the Frobenius norm reduces the problem to choosing at most $p$ nonnegative eigenvalues in the eigenbasis of $\B$.  Each retained positive eigenvalue is optimally copied, each negative eigenvalue is optimally replaced by zero, and the largest positive eigenvalues give the largest reduction in squared residual.  This is the positive-semidefinite form of low-rank spectral truncation \cite{EckartYoung1936,Mirsky1960}. Strict separation at the cutoff fixes the spectral projector; degeneracy does not. \end{proof}
This proposition corrects a common but consequential shorthand.  For an indefinite $\B$, the solution is not obtained by retaining eigenvalues of largest magnitude, nor by taking negative square roots.  It retains the largest \emph{positive} eigenvalues.
